Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["What makes the most sense?", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796071457229065*^9, 
  3.796071459985046*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"de386b51-0147-48f6-b567-\
bfe60ec75971"],

Cell["\<\
I think what makes the most sense is to approach this with an eye on Th1-Th2 \
interaction. I think that anything is just going to be unrealistic. You can \
still get bistability with a more complex immune response. Basically, if the \
system has an inherent bias towards a Th1 response, then a low initial \
parasite biomass will be insufficient to prevent Th1 polarization, whereas a \
higher parasite biomass will allow it.

Ed Schrom\[CloseCurlyQuote]s model does not include a parasite in it; instead \
it models the expression levels of transcription factors T-bet (TF1) and \
GATA-3 (TF2) and the expression levels of the cytokines IFNg (CY1) and IL4 \
(CY2). T-cells expressing T-bet are Th1 cells and T-cells expressing GATA-3 \
are Th2 cells; these cells also express the key cytokines that help to drive \
the polarization of other T-cells. That is, a naive T-cell will be bound by \
both IFNg and IL4, which will drive internal expression of T-bet and GATA-3, \
which will then drive the expression of IFNg and IL4. 

This will have to be integrated into a model that allows for dynamics of the \
T-cell populations and the parasite. That\[CloseCurlyQuote]s no small task, \
actually. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796071461863508*^9, 3.7960715684013357`*^9}, {
  3.796071641245957*^9, 3.79607168150908*^9}, {3.796071717835443*^9, 
  3.7960718986455593`*^9}, {3.796072041622864*^9, 3.7960720498340273`*^9}, {
  3.796072119573695*^9, 
  3.796072134074337*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"0d9c5a5a-3e69-4128-a1d9-\
226040420f43"],

