Cell[TextData[{
 "From Ed\[CloseCurlyQuote]s model, I have a really good idea about how to \
extend to a fully dynamic model that considers the dynamics of T cells and \
parasites. In particular, \n* the dynamics of T cells affect cell density so \
simply make cell density a dynamic variable;\n* the dynamics of the parasite \
affect APC density so simply make APC density depend on parasite biomass - \
nix: Andrea says that it is better to think of APC density as fixed - what \
changes is instead the fraction of those cells that are expressing a \
particular antigen. In other words, make Th1/Th2 antigen-presenting cells \
something like  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"APC2", 
    FractionBox["P", 
     RowBox[{"h", "+", "P"}]]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->"TraditionalForm",ExpressionUUID->
  "eafaf2b9-a309-4563-8f69-a8af94dcfaa8"],
 ", where ",
 StyleBox["P",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " is the abundance of parasites; \n* the mortality of the parasite should \
depend on both T cell density and TF expression: T cells expressing the wrong \
TF (Th1 polarized) will have little to no effect on biomass growth, whereas T \
cells expressing the right TF (Th2 polarized) will kill the parasite\n\nThe \
questions are the following: \n* How does parasite killing depend on T cell \
density? \n\nThoughts for how to structure our paper:\n- Simple model showing \
that parasite manipulation can lead to bistability between acute and chronic;\
\n- Simple model showing that Th1-Th2 cross-inhibition and self-stimulation \
can lead to bistability between Th1-biased and Th2-biased.\n- Combine simple \
models to show how parasite manipulation can lead to acute vs chronic via Th1 \
vs Th2 bias\n- Complex model based on Ed\[CloseCurlyQuote]s model showing how \
that relies on the internal \n\nQuestion for Andrea:\n- Are you happy with \
the simple model that we have for immune-mediated feedback (which currently \
only includes self-stimulation and ",
 StyleBox["self-",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "inhibition)? Can I justify the form for self-inhibition, which comes from \
Andy\[CloseCurlyQuote]s paper that is focused primarily on activation-induced \
cell death?"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.79613876943867*^9, {3.796141624151967*^9, 3.796141917854786*^9}, {
   3.796142145882575*^9, 3.7961421835354424`*^9}, {3.796142252858797*^9, 
   3.7961424980525*^9}, {3.7961461670333033`*^9, 3.7961461699342957`*^9}, {
   3.796146367170412*^9, 3.79614637146747*^9}, {3.796641733202615*^9, 
   3.796641733372972*^9}, {3.796642700716824*^9, 
   3.7966428314302*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4ad4e635-a7f9-4386-8a32-\
dc0fa5b0d997"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Th1/Th2 interference", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7966428395302887`*^9, 
  3.796642852579751*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"84cd9492-dd3e-4b75-b215-\
61490ef04270"],

Cell["\<\
What I want here is the simplest possible model of Th1/Th2 interference that \
will produce Allee effects. Note that one such model would be based on L-V \
competition models, but those models don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t always produce \
bistability. I suppose that\[CloseCurlyQuote]s the point, though, isn\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t it?

The Lotka-Volterra competition model is the following\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796643129544046*^9, 3.796643188100375*^9}, {
  3.796643242179819*^9, 
  3.7966432524502897`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"383c9a29-58c6-4eab-9401-\
3883a46840ef"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"dTh1", "=", 
   RowBox[{"r1", " ", "Th1", " ", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"1", "-", 
      FractionBox[
       RowBox[{"Th1", "+", 
        RowBox[{"a12", " ", "Th2"}]}], "K1"]}], ")"}]}]}], 
  ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"dTh2", "=", 
   RowBox[{"r2", " ", "Th2", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"1", "-", 
      FractionBox[
       RowBox[{"Th2", "+", 
        RowBox[{"a21", " ", "Th1"}]}], "K2"]}], ")"}]}]}], ";"}]}], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7966432585061817`*^9, 3.796643314247466*^9}, {
  3.796643354861796*^9, 3.796643358617669*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[668]:=",ExpressionUUID->"adfd5f27-96ae-436d-925a-abbc4e566d0a"],

Cell["Here are the equilibria of that model:", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796643367740622*^9, 
  3.7966433736242027`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"76e75a3a-e28b-4a78-8fc8-\
7b479aeefd70"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Solve", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"dTh1", "\[Equal]", "0"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"dTh2", "\[Equal]", "0"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"Th1", ",", "Th2"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796643319615232*^9, 3.796643338772966*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[670]:=",ExpressionUUID->"eec8afcd-b777-480c-9b0d-817eda48a31e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Th1", "\[Rule]", "0"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Th2", "\[Rule]", "K2"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Th1", "\[Rule]", 
      RowBox[{"-", 
       FractionBox[
        RowBox[{"K1", "-", 
         RowBox[{"a12", " ", "K2"}]}], 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"-", "1"}], "+", 
         RowBox[{"a12", " ", "a21"}]}]]}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Th2", "\[Rule]", 
      RowBox[{"-", 
       FractionBox[
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"-", "a21"}], " ", "K1"}], "+", "K2"}], 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"-", "1"}], "+", 
         RowBox[{"a12", " ", "a21"}]}]]}]}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Th1", "\[Rule]", "0"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Th2", "\[Rule]", "0"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Th1", "\[Rule]", "K1"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Th2", "\[Rule]", "0"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796643339271536*^9, 3.7966433598544617`*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[670]=",ExpressionUUID->"95cc8a8a-0a21-4bda-8b1b-fcb57c32d6c1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The Th1-biased response equilibrium is stable if a21 K1-K2 > 0; the \
Th2-biased response is stable if K1-a12 K2 < 0. Expressing those in terms of \
the antagonistic interaction parameters, bistability is possible if a21 K1 > \
K2 and a12 K2 > K1. To put those conditions biologically: the Th1 has to have \
a larger negative effect on Th2 than it has on itself, and Th2 has to have a \
larger negative effect on Th1 than it has on itself.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7966434227197*^9, 3.796643537560074*^9}, {
  3.796643672751247*^9, 
  3.796643729914255*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"64f79307-cbc5-40c2-a146-\
3a68b0894c8e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{"Eigenvalues", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"D", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"dTh1", ",", "dTh2"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"Th1", ",", "Th2"}], "}"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "/.", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Th1", "\[Rule]", "K1"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Th2", "\[Rule]", "0"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{"Eigenvalues", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"D", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"dTh1", ",", "dTh2"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"Th1", ",", "Th2"}], "}"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "/.", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Th1", "\[Rule]", "0"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"Th2", "\[Rule]", "K2"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796643376495819*^9, 3.796643417972961*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[674]:=",ExpressionUUID->"e60615d7-dcc4-4021-82cb-7cc4a32185d4"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"-", "r1"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"-", 
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"a21", " ", "K1"}], "-", "K2"}], ")"}], " ", "r2"}], 
     "K2"]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796643390083469*^9, 3.796643418371944*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[674]=",ExpressionUUID->"a7810864-6448-4e3c-b021-4c53bb68ff97"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"K1", "-", 
       RowBox[{"a12", " ", "K2"}]}], ")"}], " ", "r1"}], "K1"], ",", 
   RowBox[{"-", "r2"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796643390083469*^9, 3.7966434183772917`*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[675]=",ExpressionUUID->"dc84cc31-5b51-40db-b637-be6c1fcc8e9e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
This makes it clear why this isn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t a good model for Th1/Th2 \
responses: Th1 responses don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t have a strong negative impact \
on themselves, they have a strong negative impact on Th2 and vice versa. Thus \
we need a different set of equations.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.79664373330997*^9, 3.7966437440426188`*^9}, {
  3.796643782091564*^9, 
  3.796643819036263*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e3779dfc-ddc5-48f1-b861-\
831bf00333bb"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"dTh1", "=", 
   RowBox[{"\[Rho]1", "+", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"s1", "[", "Th1", "]"}], "Th1"}], "-", 
    RowBox[{"i1", "[", "Th2", "]"}], "-", 
    RowBox[{"m", " ", "Th1"}]}]}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"dTh2", "=", 
   RowBox[{"\[Rho]2", "+", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"s2", "[", "Th2", "]"}], "Th2"}], "-", 
    RowBox[{"i2", "[", "Th1", "]"}], "-", 
    RowBox[{"m", " ", "Th2"}]}]}], ";"}]}], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796643821740045*^9, 3.796643933460659*^9}, {
  3.796644982212834*^9, 3.7966449824675426`*^9}, {3.7966468044814177`*^9, 
  3.796646814212352*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[830]:=",ExpressionUUID->"d73d3456-0f7c-4ae0-9d09-cf2cf9c2bef0"],

Cell["Here is the Jacobian:", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.796645084000669*^9, 
