Cell["\<\
Numerical exploration shows that the same of these isoclines is such that, \
even though both isoclines always have a negative slope, it is possible for \
them to intersect in multiple places, leading to multistability.

Here is an example of such a set of parameters. With this set of parameters, \
the only stable equilibria represent polarized immune responses, so either \
Th1 or Th2 will dominate, but there is no low-activation equilibrium and no \
co-expression equilibrium. This makes sense, intuitively, given that \
cross-inhibition is stronger than self-promotion: one response must end up \
inhibited while the other dominates the immune cell population.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.797264677752122*^9, 3.79726476972066*^9}, {
   3.797264815980937*^9, 3.797264958372341*^9}, 
   3.797265902992092*^9},ExpressionUUID->"74e27719-2543-4be8-831c-\
555c9a145ea0"],

